# How It Works

**Aescia uses clinician-authored rules to turn patient-reported symptom data into a prioritized clinical worklist. No autonomous decisions. Every step auditable.**

---

## The workflow

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP 1 — PATIENT CHECK-IN                                      │
│                                                                 │
│  Patient receives a daily structured check-in via SMS or        │
│  web link. Questions are pathway-specific and clinician-        │
│  authored. Takes approximately 2–4 minutes to complete.         │
└───────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP 2 — RULES-BASED ASSESSMENT                                │
│                                                                 │
│  Responses are assessed against a rule set authored and         │
│  signed off by clinicians for that pathway. Rules are           │
│  transparent, reviewable, and configurable to local             │
│  protocols. No black-box algorithm.                             │
└───────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP 3 — URGENCY CLASSIFICATION                                │
│                                                                 │
│  Each check-in is assigned an urgency tier:                     │
│                                                                 │
│  🟢  GREEN   — Within expected parameters. No action needed.    │
│  🟡  AMBER   — Warrants clinical review at next opportunity.    │
│  🔴  RED     — Requires prompt clinical contact.                │
│                                                                 │
│  Classification logic is visible and auditable.                 │
└───────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP 4 — PRIORITIZED WORKLIST                                  │
│                                                                 │
│  The clinical team opens a worklist sorted by urgency tier.     │
│  Red cases surface first. Green cases confirm stability.        │
│  The team does not receive an uncontrolled alert stream —       │
│  they see a structured, actionable list.                        │
└───────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP 5 — CLINICAL REVIEW AND ACTION                            │
│                                                                 │
│  A clinician reviews each case and decides on the response:     │
│  phone contact, GP referral, outpatient review, emergency       │
│  pathway activation, or continue monitoring.                    │
│                                                                 │
│  Aescia does not prescribe the clinical response.               │
│  The clinician decides. The escalation pathway is the           │
│  site's own.                                                    │
└───────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  STEP 6 — AUDIT LOG                                             │
│                                                                 │
│  Every check-in response, urgency classification, clinical      │
│  review, and action taken is logged with a timestamp.           │
│  The record is available for clinical review, governance        │
│  audit, and evaluation reporting.                               │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

