## Frequently asked questions

**What if a patient doesn't complete their check-in?**
Non-completion is tracked and flagged to the clinical team. Persistently non-completing patients can be managed according to site protocol — by default, a missed check-in does not generate an automated escalation but is visible to the clinical team.

**What if the urgency rules miss something?**
No rule set is perfect. Aescia is not a safety net for all possible clinical deterioration — it is a structured follow-up tool for the complications most likely in the target pathway. The rule set is reviewed and refined through the configuration and evaluation process. Known limitations are documented.

**Can the rules be adjusted after go-live?**
Yes. Rule adjustments are subject to clinical review and sign-off before deployment. Changes are logged. The version of the rule set applied to any given check-in is part of the audit record.

**How long does a check-in take?**
Pathway-specific check-ins are designed to take 2–4 minutes. Patient burden is a design constraint, not an afterthought.

**Is this compatible with existing workflows?**
Aescia is designed to fit into existing clinical workflows rather than replace them. The worklist is a new tool; the escalation pathway is the existing one. The scoping and configuration phases are designed specifically to ensure workflow fit before go-live.

